ostBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Echo area"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/The-Echo-Area.html#The-Echo-Area","description":[{"type":"text","value":"echo area"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is the last line of the frame. Unless the\n  minibuffer is active, this is the zone that shows all the messages Emacs sends\n  to the user\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"quote-block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  The "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"echo area"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is used for displaying error messages (see "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Errors.html#Errors","description":[{"type":"text","value":"Errors"}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":"), for\n  messages made with the `message` primitive, and for echoing keystrokes. It is\n  not the same as the minibuffer, despite the fact that the minibuffer appears\n  (when active) in the same place on the screen as the echo area.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Emacs frame"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What the rest of the world calls windows. "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Frames.html","description":[{"type":"text","value":"Source"}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":".\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Emacs fringe"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On graphical displays, Emacs draws "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Fringes.html","description":[{"type":"text","value":"fringes"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"next to each window: thin\n  vertical strips down the sides which can display bitmaps indicating\n  truncation, continuation, horizontal scrolling, and so on.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Header line"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Header-Lines.html","description":[{"type":"text","value":"header line"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is like a modeline (extra information line\n  about Emacs current state), that is displayed at the top of each window\n  instead of the bottom of them.\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  Notable examples include N. Rougier displaying filename "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://github.com/rougier/elegant-emacs","description":[{"type":"text","value":"in the header line"}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":",\n  and LSP-mode powered context information ("},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacs-lsp.github.io/lsp-mode/page/main-features/#breadcrumb-on-headerline","description":[{"type":"text","value":"\"breadcrumbs\""}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":")\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"quote-block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  A window can have a "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"header line"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"at the top, just as it can have a mode line\n  at the bottom. The header line feature works just like the mode line\n  feature[…]\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Display margins"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A buffer can have blank areas called "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/elisp/Display-Margins","description":[{"type":"text","value":"display margins"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"on the\n  left and on the right. Ordinary text never appears in these areas, but you can\n  put things into the display margins using the `display` property.\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  Margins tend to be a lot larger than fringes, as margins are at least as wide\n  as characters that you'd want to display there. It is almost always disabled:\n  relevant information can be almost always also be shown in fringes, and\n  fringes take way less screen real estate.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Minibuffer"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/emacs/Minibuffer","description":[{"type":"text","value":"minibuffer"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is the buffer that takes over the last line of\n  your Emacs frame whenever Emacs prompts you (the user) for input.\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"quote-block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  A "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"minibuffer"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is a special buffer that Emacs commands use to read arguments\n  more complicated than the single numeric prefix argument. These arguments\n  include file names, buffer names, and command names (as in <kbd>M-x</kbd>).\n  The minibuffer is displayed on the bottom line of the frame, in the same place\n  as the echo area (see "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/elisp/The-Echo-Area","description":[{"type":"text","value":"The Echo Area"}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":"), but only while it is in use for reading\n  an argument.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"The Mode line"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Emacs "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/emacs/Mode-Line","description":[{"type":"text","value":"mode line"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is the status bar displayed under each\n  Emacs window. It's what vimmers know as the status line.\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"quote-block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  Each Emacs window (aside from minibuffer windows) typically has a mode line at\n  the bottom, which displays status information about the buffer displayed in\n  the window. The mode line contains information about the buffer, such as its\n  name, associated file, depth of recursive editing, and major and minor modes.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Emacs window"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/elisp/Windows","description":[{"type":"text","value":"window"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is an area of the screen that is used to display a\n  buffer (see Emacs buffer).\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"headline","level":3,"trueLevel":3,"todo":null,"priority":null,"commented":false,"tags":["category"],"title":[{"type":"text","value":"Editing"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"section","children":[{"type":"list","kind":"descriptive","children":[{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Kill"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cut (in the Cut/Copy/Paste sense)\n"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"quote-block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"  "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kill functions"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"delete text like the deletion functions, but save it so that\n  the user can reinsert it by "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"yanking"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":". Most of these functions have ‘kill-'\n  in their name. […] Killed text is saved for later yanking in the "},{"type":"bold","children":[{"type":"text","value":"kill ring"}],"postBlank":0},{"type":"text","value":".\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Major mode"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"link","linkType":"regular","pathType":"https","path":"https://emacsdocs.org/docs/emacs/Major-Modes","description":[{"type":"text","value":"major mode"}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"text","value":"is akin to file types/modes to vimmers/others. They\n  specialize Emacs for editing or interacting with particular kinds of text.\n  Each buffer has exactly one major mode at a time.\n"}],"postBlank":0}],"postBlank":1},{"type":"item","bullet":"- ","checkbox":null,"counter":null,"tag":[{"type":"text","value":"Minor mode"}],"preBlank":0,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value"